chapters.translation:
I say — may God grant us and you success — know that the virtues of tasbīḥ (glorification), taḥmīd (praise), and the remaining remembrances (adhkār) are vast, and the reports concerning them are numerous. We shall cite from them what suffices for the one who seeks guidance, if God wills.

The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: "Whoever glorifies (subbaha) after every prayer thirty-three times, and praises (ḥamida) thirty-three times, and magnifies (kabbara) thirty-three times, and completes the hundred with 'There is no god but God alone, with no partner; His is the Kingdom and His is the praise, and He is powerful over all things' — his sins are forgiven, even if they were like the foam of the sea." {«من سبح دبر كل صلاة ثلاثا وثلاثين وحمد ثلاثا وثلاثين وكبر ثلاثا وثلاثين وختم المائة بلا إله إلا الله لا شريك له له الملك وله الحمد وهو على كل شيء قدير غفرت ذنوبه ولو كانت مثل زبد البحر»}

And he, peace be upon him, said: "Whoever says 'Glory be to God and with His praise' (subḥāna Allāhi wa bi-ḥamdihi) one hundred times in a day, his sins are shed from him, even if they were like the foam of the sea." {«من قال سبحان الله وبحمده في اليوم مائة مرة حطت عنه خطاياه وإن كانت مثل زبد البحر»}

It is related that a man came to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, and said: "The world has turned away from me, and my wealth has diminished." The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "Then where are you with respect to the prayer of the angels and the glorification of all creatures, by which they are provided sustenance?" The man said: "And what is that, O Messenger of God?" He said: "Say: 'Glory be to God and with His praise; Glory be to God the Magnificent; I seek forgiveness of God' one hundred times between the break of dawn and the time you pray the morning prayer — the world will come to you humbled and subdued, and God, Mighty and Majestic, will create from every word an angel that glorifies God, Exalted is He, until the Day of Resurrection, and you shall have its reward." {«قل سبحان الله وبحمده سبحان الله العظيم استغفر الله مائة مرة ما بين طلوع الفجر إلى أن تصلي الصبح تأتيك الدنيا راغمة صاغرة ويخلق الله عز وجل من كل كلمة ملكا يسبح الله تعالى إلى يوم القيامة لك ثوابه»}

And he, peace be upon him, said: "When the servant says 'Praise be to God' (al-ḥamdu lillāh), it fills what is between the heavens and the earth; when he says 'Praise be to God' a second time, it fills what is between the seventh heaven and the lowest earth; when he says 'Praise be to God' a third time, God, Mighty and Majestic, says: 'Ask, and you shall be given.'" {«إذا قال العبد الحمد لله ملأت ما بين السماء والأرض فإذا قال الحمد لله الثانية ملأت ما بين السماء السابعة إلى الأرض السفلى فإذا قال الحمد لله الثالثة قال الله عز وجل سل تعط»}

Rifāʿah al-Zuraqī said: "One day we were praying behind the Messenger of God, peace be upon him. When he raised his head from the bowing (rukūʿ) and said 'God hears the one who praises Him' (samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah), a man behind the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: 'Our Lord, to You is the praise — abundant, good, and blessed therein.' When the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, finished his prayer, he said: 'Who was the speaker just now?' The man said: 'I, O Messenger of God.' He, peace be upon him, said: 'I saw over thirty angels vying with one another — which of them would write it first.'" {«ربنا لك الحمد حمدا كثيرا طيبا مباركا فيه ... لقد رأيت بضعة وثلاثين ملكا يبتدرونها أيهم يكتبها أولا»}

And the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "The enduring righteous deeds (al-bāqiyāt al-ṣāliḥāt) are: 'There is no god but God' (lā ilāha illā Allāh), 'Glory be to God' (subḥāna Allāh), 'Praise be to God' (al-ḥamdu lillāh), 'God is Greater' (Allāhu akbar), and 'There is no power and no strength save in God' (lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh)." {«الباقيات الصالحات هن لا إله إلا الله وسبحان الله والحمد لله والله أكبر ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله»}

And he, peace be upon him, said: "There is no man on the earth who says 'There is no god but God, and God is Greater, and Glory be to God, and Praise be to God, and there is no power and no strength save in God' — except that his sins are forgiven, even if they were like the foam of the sea." {«ما على الأرض رجل يقول لا إله إلا الله والله أكبر وسبحان الله والحمد لله ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله إلا غفرت ذنوبه ولو كانت مثل زبد البحر»} — narrated by Ibn ʿUmar.

Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr related from him, peace be upon him, that he said: "The words of remembrance that people utter concerning God's majesty, His glorification, His magnification, and His praise — they circle around the Throne, having a humming sound like the humming of bees, mentioning their companion. Would not one of you love that there should never cease to be, with God, something by which he is mentioned?" {«الذين يذكرون من جلال الله وتسبيحه وتكبيره وتحميده ينعطفن حول العرش لهن دوي كدوي النحل يذكرون بصاحبهن أو لا يحب أحدكم أن لا يزال عند الله ما يذكر به»}

Abū Hurayrah related that he, peace be upon him, said: "That I should say 'Glory be to God, and Praise be to God, and There is no god but God, and God is Greater' is more beloved to me than all that the sun has risen upon." {«لأن أقول سبحان الله والحمد لله ولا إله إلا الله والله أكبر أحب إلي مما طلعت عليه الشمس»} In another narration, he added: "And there is no power and no strength save in God," and said: "It is better than the world and all that is in it."

And he, peace be upon him, said: "The most beloved words to God, Exalted is He, are four: 'Glory be to God,' 'Praise be to God,' 'There is no god but God,' and 'God is Greater.' It will not harm you with which of them you begin." {«أحب الكلام إلى الله تعالى أربع سبحان الله والحمد لله ولا إله إلا الله والله أكبر لا يضرك بأيهن بدأت»} — narrated by Samurah ibn Jundub.

Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī related that the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, used to say: "Purity (al-ṭuhūr) is half of faith; 'Praise be to God' fills the Balance; 'Glory be to God' and 'God is Greater' fill what is between the heavens and the earth; prayer is light; charity is a proof; patience is illumination; and the Qur'an is an argument for you or against you. Every person sets out in the morning — either selling his soul and thus destroying it, or purchasing his soul and thus freeing it." {«الطهور شطر الإيمان والحمد لله تملأ الميزان وسبحان الله والله أكبر يملآن ما بين السماء والأرض والصلاة نور والصدقة برهان والصبر ضياء والقرآن حجة لك أو عليك كل الناس يغدو فبائع نفسه فموبقها أو مشتر نفسه فمعتقها»}

Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "Two words that are light upon the tongue, heavy in the Balance, and beloved to the Merciful: 'Glory be to God and with His praise; Glory be to God the Magnificent.'" {«كلمتان خفيفتان على اللسان ثقيلتان في الميزان حبيبتان إلى الرحمن سبحان الله وبحمده سبحان الله العظيم»}

Abū Dharr, may God be pleased with him, said: "I said to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him: 'Which words are most beloved to God, Mighty and Majestic?' He, peace be upon him, said: 'What God, Glorified be He, has chosen for His angels: "Glory be to God and with His praise; Glory be to God the Magnificent."'" {«ما اصطفى الله سبحانه لملائكته سبحان الله وبحمده سبحان الله العظيم»}

Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "God, Exalted is He, has chosen from words: 'Glory be to God,' 'Praise be to God,' 'There is no god but God,' and 'God is Greater.' When the servant says 'Glory be to God,' twenty good deeds are written for him and twenty evil deeds are shed from him; when he says 'God is Greater,' the like of that; and he mentioned the rest of the words." {«إن الله تعالى اصطفى من الكلام سبحان الله والحمد لله ولا إله إلا الله والله أكبر فإذا قال العبد سبحان الله كتبت له عشرون حسنة وتحط عنه عشرون سيئة وإذا قال الله أكبر فمثل ذلك»}

Jābir said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "Whoever says 'Glory be to God and with His praise' — a palm tree is planted for him in Paradise." {«من قال سبحان الله وبحمده غرست له نخلة في الجنة»}

And from Abū Dharr, may God be pleased with him, that he said: "The poor said to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him: 'The wealthy have gone off with the rewards — they pray as we pray, they fast as we fast, and they give charity from the surplus of their wealth.' He said: 'Has not God made for you that by which you may give charity? Indeed, every glorification (tasbīḥah) is a charity, every praise (taḥmīdah) is a charity, every declaration of God's oneness (tahlīlah) is a charity, every magnification (takbīrah) is a charity, commanding good is a charity, forbidding evil is a charity, and one of you placing a morsel in his family's mouth is a charity for him, and your intimacy with your spouse is a charity.' They said: 'O Messenger of God, one of us satisfies his desire and has a reward in it?' He, peace be upon him, said: 'Do you not see that if he placed it in the unlawful, would he bear a sin for it?' They said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Likewise, if he places it in the lawful, he has a reward in it.'" {«إن بكل تسبيحة صدقة وتحميدة صدقة وتهليلة صدقة وتكبيرة صدقة وأمر بمعروف صدقة ونهي عن منكر صدقة ويضع أحدكم اللقمة في في أهله فهي له صدقة وفي بضع أحدكم صدقة ... أرأيتم لو وضعها في حرام أكان عليه فيها وزر ... كذلك إن وضعها في الحلال كان له فيها أجر»}

And Abū Dharr, may God be pleased with him, said: "I said to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him: 'The people of wealth have surpassed us in reward — they say as we say, and they spend while we do not spend.' The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: 'Shall I not guide you to a deed which, if you perform it, you will overtake those before you and surpass those after you — except the one who says the like of your saying: that you glorify God after every prayer thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times?'" {«أفلا أدلك على عمل إذا أنت عملته أدركت من قبلك وفقت من بعدك إلا من قال مثل قولك تسبح الله بعد كل صلاة ثلاثا وثلاثين وتحمده ثلاثا وثلاثين وتكبر أربعا وثلاثين»}

Busrah related from the Prophet, peace be upon him, that he said: "Upon you is the tasbīḥ, the tahlīl, and the taqdīs (declaration of holiness) — do not be heedless of them, and count them upon the fingertips, for they will be made to speak." {«عليكن بالتسبيح والتهليل والتقديس فلا تغفلن واعقدن بالأنامل فإنها مستنطقات»} — meaning, by their testimony on the Day of Resurrection.

Ibn ʿUmar said: "I saw him, peace be upon him, counting the tasbīḥ upon his fingers." {«رأيته صلى الله عليه وسلم يعقد التسبيح»}

And he, peace be upon him, said — as attested by Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī: "When the servant says 'There is no god but God, and God is Greater,' God, Mighty and Majestic, says: 'My servant has spoken truly — there is no god but I, and I am Greater.' When the servant says 'There is no god but God alone, with no partner,' He, Exalted is He, says: 'My servant has spoken truly — there is no god but I alone, with no partner.' When he says 'There is no god but God, and there is no power and no strength save in God,' God, Glorified be He, says: 'My servant has spoken truly — there is no power and no strength save in Me.' Whoever says them at death, the Fire shall not touch him." {«إذا قال العبد لا إله إلا الله والله أكبر قال الله عز وجل صدق عبدي لا إله إلا أنا وأنا أكبر ... ومن قالهن عند الموت لم تمسه النار»}

Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd related from his father, from him, peace be upon him, that he said: "Is one of you unable to earn a thousand good deeds every day?" It was said: "How is that, O Messenger of God?" He, peace be upon him, said: "He glorifies God one hundred glorifications, and a thousand good deeds are written for him, and a thousand evil deeds are shed from him." {«يسبح الله مائة تسبيحة فيكتب له ألف حسنة ويحط عنه ألف سيئة»}

And he, peace be upon him, said: "O ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays — or O Abū Mūsā — shall I not guide you to a treasure from the treasures of Paradise?" He said: "Yes." He said: "Say: 'There is no power and no strength save in God.'" {«قل لا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله»} In another narration: "Shall I not teach you a word from a treasure beneath the Throne: 'There is no power and no strength save in God.'"

Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: "Shall I not guide you to a deed from the treasures of Paradise, from beneath the Throne: the saying 'There is no power and no strength save in God.' God, Exalted is He, says: 'My servant has submitted and surrendered.'" {«ألا أدلك على عمل من كنوز الجنة من تحت العرش قول لا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله يقول الله تعالى أسلم عبدي واستسلم»}

He, peace be upon him, said: "Whoever says upon rising in the morning: 'I am pleased with God as Lord, with Islam as religion, with the Qur'an as leader, and with Muḥammad, peace be upon him, as Prophet and Messenger' — it is incumbent upon God to please him on the Day of Resurrection." {«من قال حين يصبح رضيت بالله ربا وبالإسلام دينا وبالقرآن إماما وبمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم نبيا رسولا كان حقا على الله أن يرضيه يوم القيامة»} In another narration: "Whoever says that, God is pleased with him."

Mujāhid said: "When a man leaves his house and says 'In the name of God' (bismillāh), the angel says: 'You have been guided.' When he says 'I have placed my trust in God' (tawakkaltu ʿalā Allāh), the angel says: 'You have been sufficed.' When he says 'There is no power and no strength save in God,' the angel says: 'You have been protected.' Then the devils disperse from him, saying: 'What do you want from a man who has been guided, sufficed, and protected? You have no way to him.'" {«إذا خرج الرجل من بيته فقال بسم الله قال الملك هديت فإذا قال توكلت على الله قال الملك كفيت وإذا قال لا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله قال الملك وقيت فتتفرق عنه الشياطين فيقولون ما تريدون من رجل قد هدي وكفي ووقي لا سبيل لكم إليه»}

If you say: "How is it that the remembrance of God, Glorified be He — despite its lightness upon the tongue and the lack of toil in it — has become more excellent and more beneficial than the whole of the acts of worship, despite the multiplicity of hardships in them?" — Know that the verification of this is not fitting except for the science of unveiling (ʿilm al-mukāshafah).

And the measure that may be mentioned in the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah) is that the effective and beneficial thing is remembrance upon constancy (al-dhikr ʿalā al-dawām) with the heart's presence. As for remembrance by the tongue while the heart is heedless, it is of little avail — and in the reports there is what indicates this as well. {«فإن قلت فما بال ذكر الله سبحانه مع خفته على اللسان وقلة التعب فيه صار أفضل وأنفع من جملة العبادات مع كثرة المشقات فيها فاعلم أن تحقيق هذا لا يليق إلا بعلم المكاشفة والقدر الذي يسمح بذكره في علم المعاملة أن المؤثر النافع هو الذكر على الدوام مع حضور القلب فأما الذكر باللسان والقلب لاه فهو قليل الجدوى»}

And the heart's presence with God, Exalted is He, in a single moment of remembrance, while heedlessness of God, Mighty and Majestic, with occupation in the world, is also of little avail. Rather, the heart's presence with God, Exalted is He, upon constancy — or in most of the times — takes precedence over the acts of worship; indeed, by it all other acts of worship are ennobled, and it is the ultimate fruit of the practical acts of worship.

For remembrance has a beginning and an end. Its beginning necessitates intimacy (uns) and love (ḥubb) for God; its end necessitates intimacy and love, and it issues from it. And the sought goal is that intimacy and love. For the seeker (murīd) at the start of his affair may be burdened with turning his heart and tongue from whisperings (waswās) to the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic. If he is granted success in constancy, he becomes intimate with it, and love of the Remembered is planted in his heart. One should not wonder at this, for it is observed in customs that if one mentions an absent person not present before a man, and repeatedly mentions his qualities in his presence, he comes to love him — indeed, one may fall in love through description and much remembrance. Then, when he has fallen in love through the initially burdensome abundance of remembrance, he becomes compelled to the abundance of remembrance at the end, such that he cannot bear to be without it. For whoever loves a thing mentions it much.

And whoever mentions a thing much — even if initially burdensome — comes to love it. Likewise, the beginning of remembrance is burdensome until it yields the fruit of intimacy with the Remembered and love for Him; then, at the end, patience without it becomes impossible, so the cause becomes a cause and the fruit becomes fruitful.

This is the meaning of the saying of some of them: "I struggled against the Qur'an for twenty years, then I delighted in it for twenty years." And delight issues only from intimacy and love, and intimacy issues only from constancy in struggle and burden over a long period, until the burden becomes a nature. How can this be deemed remote, when a man may burden himself to eat food he finds repugnant at first, struggling to consume it and persisting in it, until it becomes agreeable to his nature such that he cannot bear to be without it? For the soul is accustomed to and endures what it burdens itself with.

The soul is what you accustom it to — it becomes accustomed...
Meaning: whatever you burden it with at first becomes a nature for it at the end.

Then, when intimacy with the remembrance of God, Glorified be He, is attained, one is cut off from other than the remembrance of God. And whatever is other than God, Mighty and Majestic, is that which departs from him at death — so there remains with him in the grave neither family, nor wealth, nor child, nor authority; there remains only the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic.

If he has become intimate with it, he delights in it and takes pleasure in the cessation of the obstacles that diverted him from it — for the necessities of needs in the life of this world divert one from the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic, and after death there remains no obstacle. It is as if he has been left alone with his Beloved, so his joy is immense, and he is delivered from the prison in which he was prevented from that which was his intimacy. For this reason he, peace be upon him, said: "The Holy Spirit breathed into my heart: 'Love whom you will, for you shall part from him.'" {«إن روح القدس نفث في روعي أحبب من أحببت فإنك مفارقه»} He meant by this everything connected to the world, for that perishes in his regard at death — {«كل من عليها فان ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام»} — and the world perishes at death in his regard, until it perishes in itself when the Book's term is reached.

And this intimacy is that which the servant delights in after his death, until he descends into the vicinity of God, Mighty and Majestic, and ascends from remembrance to the Vision (al-liqāʾ) — and that is after what is in the graves is scattered and what is in the breasts is obtained. And one should not deny the persistence of the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic, with him at death, saying: "Death annihilates him — how then does the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic, remain with him?" For he is not annihilated with an annihilation that prevents remembrance; rather, he is annihilated from the world and the realm of dominion and testimony (ʿālam al-mulk wa al-shahādah), not from the realm of sovereignty (ʿālam al-malakūt).

And to what we have mentioned is the allusion in his saying, peace be upon him: "The grave is either a pit from the pits of the Fire or a garden from the gardens of Paradise." {«القبر إما حفرة من حفر النار أو روضة من رياض الجنة»} And in his saying, peace be upon him: "The souls of the martyrs are in the crops of green birds." {«أرواح الشهداء في حواصل طيور خضر»} And in his saying, peace be upon him, to the slain of Badr from the polytheists: "O so-and-so, O so-and-so" — and the Prophet, peace be upon him, named them — "Have you found what your Lord promised you to be true? For I have found what my Lord promised me to be true." {«يا فلان ويا فلان هل وجدتم ما وعد ربكم حقا فإني وجدت ما وعدني ربي حقا»} ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, heard his saying, peace be upon him, and said: "O Messenger of God, how do they hear, and how do they answer, when they have become putrid?" He, peace be upon him, said: "By Him in whose hand is my soul, you are not more hearing of my words than they — but they are not able to answer." This hadith is in the Ṣaḥīḥ — this is his saying, peace be upon him, concerning the polytheists. As for the believers and the martyrs, he, peace be upon him, said: "Their souls are in the crops of green birds, suspended beneath the Throne." {«أرواحهم في حواصل طيور خضر معلقة تحت العرش»} And this state, and what these words allude to, does not negate the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic. And He, Exalted is He, said: {«ولا تحسبن الذين قتلوا في سبيل الله أمواتا بل أحياء عند ربهم يرزقون فرحين بما آتاهم الله من فضله ويستبشرون بالذين لم يلحقوا بهم من خلفهم»} — to the end of the verse.

And for the honor of the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic, the rank of martyrdom (shahādah) is magnified — because the sought goal is the final state (al-khātīmah). And by "final state" we mean bidding farewell to the world and arriving before God with the heart wholly absorbed in God, Mighty and Majestic, cut off from all attachments to other than Him.

For if a servant is able to make his concern wholly absorbed in God, Mighty and Majestic, he is not able to die in that state except in the ranks of combat.

For he has cut off hope from his lifeblood, his family, his wealth, his child — indeed, from the whole world, for he desires it only for his life, and his life has become light in his heart through love of God, Mighty and Majestic, and seeking His good pleasure. There is no self-emptying for God greater than this. For this reason the matter of martyrdom is magnified, and there has come concerning it virtues beyond counting.

From that: When ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Anṣārī was martyred on the day of Uḥud, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Jābir: "Shall I not give you glad tidings, O Jābir?" He said: "Yes, may God give you glad tidings of good." He said: "Indeed, God, Mighty and Majestic, has revived your father and seated him before Him, with no veil between him and Him. He, Exalted is He, said: 'Ask of Me, My servant, whatever you wish, and I shall give you.' He said: 'O Lord, return me to the world so that I may be killed in You and in Your Prophet another time.' He, Mighty and Majestic, said: 'The decree has preceded from Me that they shall not return to it.'" {«إن الله عز وجل أحيا أباك فأقعده بين يديه وليس بينه وبينه ستر فقال تعالى تمن علي يا عبدي ما شئت أعطيكه فقال يا رب إن تردني إلى الدنيا حتى أقتل فيك وفي نبيك مرة أخرى فقال عز وجل سبق القضاء مني بأنهم إليها لا يرجعون»}

Then killing is a cause of the final state upon such a condition — for if he had not been killed and had remained for a period, perhaps the desires of the world would have returned to him and overcome what had taken hold of his heart of the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic.

For this reason, the fear of the people of knowledge (ahl al-maʿrifah) concerning the final state is great — for the heart, even if it is bound to the remembrance of God, Mighty and Majestic, is volatile, not free from turning toward the desires of the world, nor free from a languor that befalls it.

So if, at the final moment, a matter of the world is represented in his heart and overcomes him, and he departs from the world in that state, it is likely that its dominance over him will persist, and he will yearn for it after death and wish to return to the world.

And that is due to the smallness of his share in the Hereafter — for a man dies upon what he lived, and is resurrected upon what he died.

So the safest of states from this danger is the final state of martyrdom — for the martyr's intention was not the attainment of wealth, nor that it be said "brave," nor other than that, {«كما ورد به الخبر»} — rather, love of God, Mighty and Majestic, and the elevation of His word. This state is that which is expressed by: {«إن الله اشترى من المؤمنين أنفسهم وأموالهم بأن لهم الجنة»} And such a person is the seller of the world for the Hereafter.

And the state of the martyr corresponds to the meaning of your saying {«لا إله إلا الله»} — for he has no object of intention other than God, Mighty and Majestic. And every object of intention is a worshipped one (maʿbūd), and every worshipped one is a god (ilāh). So this martyr is, by the tongue of his state (lisān al-ḥāl), saying {«لا إله إلا الله»} — for he has no object of intention other than Him.

And whoever says it by his tongue while his state does not assist him — his affair is in the will of God, Mighty and Majestic, and the danger is not secure in his regard.

For this reason, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, preferred the saying "There is no god but God" over the rest of the remembrances, {«وذكر ذلك مطلقا في مواضع الترغيب ثم ذكر في بعض المواضع الترغيب ثم ذكر في بعض المواضع الصدق والإخلاص فقال مرة من قال لا إله إلا الله مخلصا ومعنى الإخلاص مساعدة الحال للمقال»} — and he mentioned it absolutely in places of encouragement, then in some places he mentioned encouragement, then in some places he mentioned truthfulness and sincerity (ikhlāṣ), saying once: "Whoever says 'There is no god but God' sincerely" — and the meaning of sincerity is the assistance of the state to the statement.

So we ask God, Exalted is He, to make us, in the final state, of the people of "There is no god but God" — in state and in statement, outwardly and inwardly — so that we bid farewell to the world not turning toward it, but rather weary of it and loving the meeting with God. For whoever loves the meeting with God, God loves his meeting; and whoever dislikes the meeting with God, God dislikes his meeting. {«من أحب لقاء الله تعالى أحب الله لقاءه ومن كره لقاء الله كره الله لقاءه»} These are allusions to the meanings of remembrance, which cannot be exceeded in the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah).
